events.content:
The plugin renderer container (`.dashkit-item__renderer`) is `position:absolute; inset:0` — so it fills the grid item. My widget root with `height: 100%` will work.

Also note `.dashkit-grid-item__item_editMode` sets `pointer-events: none` — in edit mode widget content is not interactive (overlay captures events). Good — that's dashkit standard.

Now, plugin renderer is a React component receiving PluginWidgetProps. For charts inside dashkit, resize → Chart adapts (it uses ResizeObserver presumably; @gravity-ui/charts Chart adapts to parent size). Good.

Now write the code. Structure:

**src/Root.tsx** — theme state + ThemeProvider + App.

**src/main.tsx** — keep uikit CSS imports; add RGL CSS imports; render Root. Also `configure({lang: Lang.En})` from uikit (docs require setting language for dashkit). Put configure in main.tsx or dashboard setup module. I'll put it in `src/dashkit-setup.ts` imported by App... it must run before DashKit renders — module import side effect is fine.

**src/dashboard/setup.ts**:
```ts
import {configure, Lang} from '@gravity-ui/uikit';
import {DashKit, pluginText, pluginTitle} from '@gravity-ui/dashkit';
import {StatWidget} from '../widgets/StatWidget';
import {ChartWidget} from '../widgets/ChartWidget';

configure({lang: Lang.En});

DashKit.setSettings({
  gridLayout: {margin: [8, 8], rowHeight: 24}, // cols default 36
});

DashKit.registerPlugins(
  pluginTitle,
  pluginText,
  {
    type: 'stat',
    defaultLayout: {w: 9, h: 4, minW: 6, minH: 3},
    renderer: StatWidget,  // signature (props, forwardedRef) => ReactNode; a plain function component works
  },
  {
    type: 'chart',
    defaultLayout: {w: 18, h: 10, minW: 9, minH: 6},
    renderer: ChartWidget,
  },
);
```

Wait — renderer signature: `(props: P, forwardedRef) => React.ReactNode`. A React FC `(props) => JSX` matches (ignores second arg). TS: Plugin<P extends PluginWidgetProps<T> = any...> — registerPlugins(...plugins: Plugin[]). Plugin['renderer'] with default any props — passing FC<PluginWidgetProps<...>> should be assignable. Let me be careful with types: `renderer: (props: P, forwardedRef) => ReactNode` where P = any by default in Plugin (Plugin<P extends PluginWidgetProps<T> = any, T = StringParams>). So any function component is assignable.

Hmm — one concern: `pluginText` requires an apiHandler for markdown? Default pluginText without setSettings renders plain text? The docs example sets apiHandler to fetch markdown. Without it, text renders as-is (there's a default handler probably that returns text). Let me check plugins/Text to see default behavior — if no apiHandler, does it render raw text? Let me check Text.js quickly.

Also `pluginTitle` data: `{size: 'm', text, showInTOC}`.

**Widget data model**: For stat: `data: {title: string, value: string, delta: number, ...}`. For chart: `data: {title: string, series: 'revenue'|'orders', chartType: 'line'|'bar-x'}` or just embed the full chart data in config. Simplest: embed actual chart data config into item.data. Config must be JSON-serializable — chart data is plain objects, fine.

**Action panel items** (in App):
```tsx
const actionItems: ActionPanelItem[] = [
  {id: 'title', icon: <Icon data={Heading}/>, title: 'Title', dragProps: {type: 'title'}, onClick: () => addWidget('title')},
  ...
];
```
Hmm — ActionPanelItem has both onClick and dragProps optional. If both provided? onClick takes priority for clicks; dragProps enables drag. Look at ActionPanel.js to see behavior... Let me check quickly whether having both works.

For click-to-add I need `addWidget(type)` using DashKit.setItem with plugin defaultLayout. For drop I use onDrop with dropProps.newLayout as updateLayout.

**onChange**: `(data) => setConfig(data.config)`.

**onDrop**: 
```ts
const onDrop = (dropProps: ItemDropProps) => {
  const type = dropProps.dragProps.type;
  const newConfig = DashKit.setItem({
    item: {type, namespace: 'default', data: defaultDataFor(type), layout: {...dropProps.itemLayout}}, 
    config,
    options: {updateLayout: dropProps.newLayout},
  });
  setConfig(newConfig);
  dropProps.commit();
};
```
Note item.layout is `Omit<ConfigLayout,'i'>`. dropProps.itemLayout is ConfigLayout (has i: TEMPORARY_ITEM_ID presumably). I should omit `i` when passing as item.layout? The docs example: `layout: {h: 6, w: 12, x: 0, y: 2}` (no i). dropProps.itemLayout.i would be the temp id "__dropping-elem__". Passing it might be wrong. Safer: pass `{w, h, x, y}` picked from itemLayout, plus updateLayout = newLayout. Let me check how setItem uses item.layout + updateLayout... In dashkit's updateManager/RegisterManager: when adding an item with layout + updateLayout, the temp item in newLayout gets replaced. Let me check the helper to get this right — check utils updateManager or the setItem implementation.

Let me read the relevant part of UpdateManager.

